{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>By the sweat of your brow you shall eat bread.</b> There is support from here for the words of the physicians who say that before every meal a person should engage in some labor that warms those who perform it, for this aids digestion, as it is said (Psalms 128:2): ״יְגִיעַ כַּפֶּיךָ כִּי תֹאכֵל אַשְׁרֶיךָ וְטוֹב לָךְ״. This is what it says: ״בְּעִצָּבוֹן תֹּאכְלֶנָּה כֹּל יְמֵי חַיֶּיךָ״ (Genesis 3:17) — for עִצָּבוֹן is an expression of labor, as it says in Job (10:8): ״יָדֶיךָ עִצְּבוּנִי וַיַּעֲשׂוּנִי״. And in saying ״כֹּל יְמֵי חַיֶּיךָ״, He indicated that he shall eat only enough for his livelihood and not pursue superfluities, for what advantage does he have that he should labor for the wind."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:26:43.744651707Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}